---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { site } from "../../data/site";

// Linked from App Store Connect, the app's paywall, and the site footer. Moved here
// from lillyseay.com/hiking-passport/privacy, which now redirects to this page. Every
// statement below was checked against the app source at ~/Developer/Hiking-Passport.
// Do not edit a claim here without checking again.
const updated = "September 15, 2026";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy, Hiking Passport"
  description="How Hiking Passport handles your Apple Health data, your passport, and your purchases."
>
  <section class="post legal">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="post-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Hiking Passport</p>
        <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          There is no account and no cloud. This page spells out exactly what
          Hiking Passport reads, the one thing that leaves your iPhone, and how
          to turn any of it off.
        </p>
        <p class="org">Last updated: {updated}</p>
      </div>

      <div class="post-body">
        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <p>
          Hiking Passport is an iPhone app that turns the hikes you are chasing
          into a passport you fill in over a season. It builds a training plan
          from your own activity, and shows your progress as a drawn scene
          rather than a chart.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is no account to create and no login. I do not run a server, and
          I never sell anyone's personal information. Your passport, your hikes,
          your notes, and your photos are stored on your iPhone and nowhere
          else.
        </p>

        <h2>What the app reads from Apple Health</h2>
        <p>
          The app asks permission to read five things from Apple Health. You
          choose what to allow, and you can change your mind at any time.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Steps</li>
          <li>Walking and running distance</li>
          <li>Flights climbed</li>
          <li>Active energy</li>
          <li>Workouts</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          That is the whole list. The app reads nothing else from Apple Health,
          and it never writes anything back. It does not read your heart rate,
          your sleep, your cardio fitness, or your body measurements. If you
          decline a category, or skip Apple Health entirely, the app still works
          and you log your hiking by hand.
        </p>
        <p>
          What it reads stays on your iPhone. It is used to fill in your season
          and to size your training plan, and it is cached on the device so the
          app works offline.
        </p>

        <h2>How your plan is generated</h2>
        <p>
          Your training plan is built by rules that run on your iPhone. It is
          not generated by an AI model, and nothing about your training is sent
          anywhere to produce it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Apple Intelligence is used in three small places, and only ever to
          write words: looking up a trail's published statistics when you ask it
          to, naming your plan, and putting your hiking buddy's daily note in
          their own voice. These run on your iPhone using Apple's on-device
          model. Hiking Passport does not send them to Apple's Private Cloud
          Compute or to any other server. Turn Apple Intelligence off and the
          app keeps working, with the same plan.
        </p>

        <h2>What you tell the app directly</h2>
        <p>
          Setup asks what you need the plan to work around, and you can tell it
          about an injury, a disability, a hard schedule, money, transport, or
          anything else that shapes how you train. That is sensitive information
          and it is treated that way. It is stored on your iPhone, it is used on
          your iPhone, and it is never sent anywhere.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same is true of everything else you enter: your hikes, your summit
          dates, your ratings and moods, your notes and journal entries, and any
          photo you attach to a summit.
        </p>

        <h2>Apple Watch and widgets</h2>
        <p>
          If you use the Apple Watch app or the Home Screen widgets, the app
          shares a copy of your passport and your plan with them on your own
          devices, so they can display it and keep working offline. That copy
          moves between your iPhone and your Apple Watch directly. It does not
          pass through me.
        </p>

        <h2>No sync and no backup</h2>
        <p>
          Hiking Passport does not use iCloud and does not sync. A passport
          lives on the iPhone that made it, and a new iPhone starts empty. The
          upside is that nothing of yours is stored anywhere I could reach.
        </p>

        <h2>Purchases</h2>
        <p>
          Subscriptions are handled by Apple and by{" "}
          <a href="https://www.revenuecat.com/privacy/">RevenueCat</a>, which
          keeps track of whether your subscription is active. This is the only
          network connection the app makes. RevenueCat receives an anonymous
          identifier for your install, your purchase and receipt status, and
          basic device and platform information. It does not receive your name,
          your email, your Apple Health data, your hikes, or your photos.
        </p>
        <p>Apple handles the payment itself. I never see your card details.</p>

        <h2>Analytics and tracking</h2>
        <p>
          There are none. Hiking Passport contains no analytics and no crash
          reporting service, it does not track you, and it does not collect data
          for advertising or measurement across apps or websites. Apple may
          share aggregate crash and usage reports with me through App Store
          Connect if you have opted into sharing those with developers in your
          iOS settings. Those come from Apple, not from anything in the app, and
          they do not identify you.
        </p>

        <h2>Your choices</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Turn off Health access.</strong> Open the Health app, tap your
            picture, then Privacy, then Apps, then Hiking Passport. Turn off any category,
            or all of them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Turn off Apple Intelligence.</strong> In iOS Settings, under Apple
            Intelligence. The app keeps working and your plan does not change.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Delete everything.</strong> Deleting the app removes the data
            it stored on your iPhone. Your Apple Health data belongs to Apple Health
            and stays there.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Ask me to delete a purchase record.</strong> Email me and I will
            have RevenueCat delete the record tied to your install.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Children</h2>
        <p>
          Hiking Passport is not directed to children under 13, and I do not
          knowingly collect their information. If you think a child gave me
          theirs, email me and I will delete it.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>
          If the app starts handling your information differently, I will update
          this page and change the date at the top. The version you are reading
          was last updated on {updated}.
        </p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Questions, deletion requests, or anything else about your privacy:{
            " "
          }
          <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>. Hiking Passport is
          made by Lilly Seay in Portland, Oregon.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
